  "definitions": {
    "priceToIncome": "OECD nominal house price index for Australia divided by ABS average weekly earnings, all employees, persons. The numerator is an index, so the level is index points per dollar of weekly earnings.",
    "priceToRent": "OECD nominal house price index divided by the OECD rent price index. The ratio is the inverse of the gross rental yield and the housing equivalent of a price-to-earnings multiple. A net cap rate after vacancy, operating costs and depreciation would be lower, but the expense ratio is stable over long periods, so the ranking is similar. The series begins in 1972; the figure starts in 1990 to align with price-to-income, and the earlier observations do not change the percentile or the destinations.",
    "ladder": "Each row holds earnings and rents at their current levels and moves only the price of the dwelling stock. The 0% row uses 2026-Q1 for price-to-income and 2025-Q3 for price-to-rent."
  },
